    <body>
      <sec id="article-23201.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Primary hyperparathyroidism (PHPT) is a common endocrine disorder caused by autonomous parathyroid hormone secretion, leading to hypercalcemia and systemic complications involving bone, kidney, and neurocognitive function. This activity reviews the etiology, epidemiology, pathophysiology, and the varying clinical presentations of PHPT. Most cases arise from a single benign adenoma, with less frequent multigland disease or carcinoma. Widespread biochemical screening has shifted the most frequent presentation from classic symptomatic disease to predominantly asymptomatic hypercalcemia, including normocalcemic and normohormonal variants.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Additionally, this course outlines the diagnostic evaluation, which relies on biochemical confirmation of elevated or inappropriately normal PTH, with imaging reserved for surgical planning and evidence-based management of PHPT, including criteria for parathyroidectomy, intraoperative PTH monitoring, and postoperative care, as well as nonsurgical options. Participants&#x000a0;will also gain an understanding of typical and atypical presentations, interpreting laboratory findings, differentiating PHPT from secondary causes, and determining appropriate referral for surgical or medical management. This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in identifying the PHPT, performing the recommended evaluation, reducing complications such as bone loss and nephrolithiasis, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach when managing this condition to&#x000a0;support patient safety, optimize outcomes, and enhance long-term monitoring and care coordination in clinical practice.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Evaluate the&#x000a0;diagnostic findings associated with primary hyperparathyroidism.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate primary hyperparathyroidism from secondary causes of hypercalcemia based on key diagnostic features.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based criteria to determine appropriate candidates for parathyroidectomy in patients with primary hyperparathyroidism.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with interprofessional team members to coordinate comprehensive care and optimize long-term outcomes for individuals with primary hyperparathyroidism.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=23201&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=23201">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Primary hyperparathyroidism (PHPT) is an endocrine disorder defined by autonomous overproduction of parathyroid hormone (PTH), resulting in hypercalcemia and disrupted calcium homeostasis. The most common etiology is a single benign adenoma, while multigland hyperplasia, multiple adenomas, and parathyroid carcinoma are less frequent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r1">[1]</xref> Variants, eg, normocalcemic and normohormonal PHPT, represent milder biochemical manifestations of the same disease process. PHPT is the leading cause of hypercalcemia in the outpatient setting, predominantly affecting postmenopausal women. The incidence of PHPT is rising due to increased routine biochemical screening. The underlying pathophysiology involves a shift in the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) set point, causing persistent PTH secretion despite elevated serum calcium. This leads to increased bone resorption, renal calcium reabsorption, and intestinal calcium absorption.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Histopathologically, parathyroid adenomas are well-circumscribed lesions composed primarily of chief cells with reduced stromal fat. Hyperplasia is characterized by diffuse enlargement of all&#x000a0;4 glands, whereas carcinoma is identified by capsular or vascular invasion. The clinical presentation has shifted from classic symptomatic disease, such as nephrolithiasis, bone pain, and fractures, to predominantly asymptomatic hypercalcemia detected incidentally. Diagnostic evaluation relies exclusively on biochemical testing, focusing on confirming elevated or inappropriately elevated PTH in the presence of hypercalcemia after secondary causes have been excluded. Imaging studies for localization are reserved solely for surgical planning.</p>
        <p>Parathyroidectomy remains the only curative treatment, with cure rates exceeding 95%. Medical management and observation are suitable for selected patients who are asymptomatic or considered poor surgical candidates. Prognosis is excellent with timely surgical intervention, as both bone density and renal function typically improve, and recurrence is rare. Patients managed conservatively often remain stable for years with appropriate monitoring. Untreated PHPT can result in complications, eg, osteoporosis, nephrolithiasis, neuropsychiatric symptoms, and cardiovascular changes. Surgical complications, including transient hypocalcemia, hungry bone syndrome, or recurrent laryngeal nerve injury, are uncommon and generally manageable by experienced clinicians.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r5">[5]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Classic Primary Hyperparathyroidism</bold>
</p>
        <p>PHPT is characterized by autonomous overproduction of PTH that occurs independently of normal calcium feedback regulation,&#x000a0;leading to hypercalcemia. The disorder arises from clonal dysregulation and overgrowth of parathyroid tissue, most commonly involving a single gland. Reduced expression or altered function of the CaSR contributes to this loss of calcium-mediated inhibition.</p>
        <p>Under normal physiology, activation of the CaSR on parathyroid chief cells suppresses PTH secretion, PTH gene expression, and cellular proliferation. In PHPT, the set point for calcium-induced inhibition of PTH release is shifted upward, resulting in higher circulating calcium levels required to suppress PTH secretion. PTH hypersecretion subsequently promotes bone resorption, renal tubular calcium reabsorption, and increased intestinal calcium absorption via enhanced renal production of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, while simultaneously reducing renal phosphate reabsorption.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>PHPT ranks as the third most prevalent endocrine disorder, following diabetes mellitus and thyroid disease. The etiological distribution is as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Single parathyroid adenoma: 80% to 85%</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Multigland disease (hyperplasia or multiple adenomas): 10% to 15%</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Parathyroid carcinoma: &#x0003c;1%</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Atypical parathyroid adenomas exhibit histologic features suggestive of malignancy but lack evidence of invasive growth and are therefore classified within the benign disease spectrum. Multigland disease occurs more frequently in hereditary syndromes, eg, multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1), multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A (MEN2A), and <italic toggle="yes">CDC73</italic>-related disorders, as well as in lithium-associated PHPT.&#x000a0;(Please refer to the <bold>Differential Diagnosis</bold>&#x000a0;section for more information.)&#x000a0;Although most adenomas arise in orthotopic parathyroid glands, up to 10% are found in ectopic locations. Common ectopic sites include the thymus, thyroid gland, pericardium, retroesophageal space, and superior mediastinum. Rare sites comprise the pharynx, lateral neck, and esophagus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Normocalcemic Primary Hyperparathyroidism</bold>
</p>
        <p>Normocalcemic primary hyperparathyroidism (NPHPT) is a variant of primary hyperparathyroidism defined by persistently elevated PTH levels with normal total and ionized serum calcium concentrations, confirmed by repeated measurements over at least&#x000a0;3 months. This disorder results from autonomous parathyroid overactivity, most often due to a single adenoma or, less frequently, multigland hyperplasia, but without sufficient PTH excess to induce overt hypercalcemia.</p>
        <p>The underlying pathophysiology mirrors that of classical hypercalcemic PHPT, involving clonal dysregulation of parathyroid cells and reduced CaSR expression. While many patients are asymptomatic, some may present with nephrolithiasis or osteoporosis. The clinical course is variable, and only a minority of cases progress to hypercalcemia over several years. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23200">Normocalcemic Hyperparathyroidism</ext-link>," for further information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Normohormonal Primary Hyperparathyroidism</bold>
</p>
        <p>Normohormonal primary hyperparathyroidism (NH-PHPT) is defined by elevated serum calcium levels with PTH concentrations that remain within the normal reference range. NH-PHPT results from autonomous parathyroid overactivity, most frequently due to a single adenoma, although multigland disease is more common in this variant than in classic hyperparathyroidism. PTH levels are described as &#x0201c;inappropriately normal&#x0201d; because they do not suppress in response to hypercalcemia. The pathophysiology of&#x000a0;NH-PHPT is similar to that of classical PHPT, involving clonal proliferation of parathyroid cells and altered CaSR responsiveness, which leads to impaired calcium-mediated feedback inhibition. Patients typically exhibit milder biochemical abnormalities but may still develop osteoporosis, nephrolithiasis, or other end-organ complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>PHPT represents the most common identifiable cause of hypercalcemia in ambulatory patients, particularly among postmenopausal women.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r1">[1]</xref> The female-to-male ratio is approximately 3:1 to 4:1, with peak incidence occurring between ages 50 and 60.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r15">[15]</xref> In the United States, the age-adjusted prevalence is estimated at 233 per 100,000 women and 85 per 100,000 men. Incidence rates are higher among Black individuals compared to White populations, and lower among Asians and Hispanics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>The widespread adoption of automated biochemical screening and routine serum calcium testing has shifted the clinical profile of PHPT from predominantly symptomatic presentations, such as nephrolithiasis, osteitis fibrosa cystica, and bone pain, to mainly asymptomatic or mild hypercalcemic forms. In regions with limited laboratory screening and less frequent vitamin D repletion, PHPT continues to present as symptomatic disease with skeletal or renal complications. The normocalcemic variant of PHPT has become increasingly recognized as PTH assays are more frequently utilized during evaluations for osteoporosis or nephrolithiasis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>PTH, produced by the chief cells of the parathyroid glands, regulates extracellular calcium homeostasis through feedback mechanisms mediated by the CaSR. A decrease in serum ionized calcium reduces CaSR activity, which stimulates PTH secretion. PTH subsequently increases bone resorption, enhances distal tubular calcium reabsorption, and activates renal 1-&#x003b1;-hydroxylase, thereby promoting intestinal calcium absorption via 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In primary hyperparathyroidism, clonal overactivity of parathyroid tissue elevates the set-point for calcium-mediated inhibition, resulting in persistent PTH secretion despite hypercalcemia. The typical biochemical profile includes elevated serum calcium, low or low-normal serum phosphate, and variable hypercalciuria due to increased filtered calcium load exceeding tubular reabsorption capacity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>PHPT most commonly presents as a solitary parathyroid adenoma, typically well-circumscribed, soft, and encapsulated, and usually weighing less than 1 g. Grossly, these adenomas are reddish-brown or tan, exhibit a smooth surface, and display a thin rim of compressed normal parathyroid tissue at the periphery. Multiglandular hyperplasia, involving all&#x000a0;4 glands, is less common and manifests as diffusely enlarged, nonencapsulated glands. Although rare, parathyroid carcinoma is generally larger, firm, gray-white, and adherent to adjacent structures.</p>
        <p>Microscopically, parathyroid adenomas are primarily composed of chief cells arranged in solid, trabecular, or acinar patterns, with a significant reduction in stromal fat relative to normal glands. Oxyphil cell adenomas are rare but can occur. Chronic excess of PTH results in osteoclastic bone resorption, cortical thinning, fibrosis, and cystic degeneration. In advanced cases, osteitis fibrosa cystica may develop, which is characterized by fibroblast proliferation, multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells, and brown hemosiderin deposits, known as &#x0201c;brown tumors.&#x0201d;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>Parathyroid carcinoma is histologically defined by capsular, vascular, or perineural invasion, a trabecular growth pattern, and increased mitotic activity. These features are often accompanied by dense fibrous bands that separate tumor lobules. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/26659">Parathyroid Cancer</ext-link>," for further information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r20">[20]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Historically, primary hyperparathyroidism was most often diagnosed in patients presenting with nephrolithiasis, osteoporosis, or pathologic fractures. Since the 1970s, the widespread adoption of automated serum chemistry panels has shifted detection toward asymptomatic disease, with most cases now identified through incidental findings of hypercalcemia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;Diagnosis currently relies on biochemical confirmation, demonstrated by elevated or inappropriately normal parathyroid hormone levels in the presence of hypercalcemia.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical History</bold>
</p>
        <p>During medical history assessment, clinicians should evaluate for renal calculi, bone pain, myalgias or muscle weakness, neurocognitive symptoms (eg, fatigue, memory changes, or depression), gastrointestinal discomfort, and medication use, including thiazide diuretics, lithium, calcium supplements, vitamin B supplements, and vitamin D preparations. Despite the absence of overt symptoms in many patients, up to 55% demonstrate subclinical nephrocalcinosis or nonobstructing renal stones. Among symptomatic patients, up to 75% present with acute renal colic or nephrolithiasis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Physical Examination</bold>
</p>
        <p>Physical examination findings are frequently unremarkable, but should emphasize identifying complications related to hypercalcemia or alternative etiologies. Palpable parathyroid masses occur infrequently; however, a firm, fixed cervical mass in the setting of hypercalcemia warrants concern for parathyroid carcinoma. Associated manifestations of hyperparathyroidism and hypercalcemia include bone or joint pain, fractures, osteoporosis, muscle weakness, fatigue, abdominal pain, anorexia, constipation, depression, cognitive impairment, sleep disturbance, hypertension, cardiac arrhythmias, left ventricular hypertrophy, nephrolithiasis, nephrocalcinosis, and polyuria.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Patients presenting with clinical features or diagnostic findings of hypercalcemia should undergo evaluation for PHPT through laboratory testing (see <bold>Table 1</bold>). Diagnosis is confirmed when elevated serum calcium (total or ionized) coincides with an inappropriately high or normal PTH level measured simultaneously. Hypercalcemia is defined as total calcium greater than 10.2 mg/dL (2.55 mmol/L) or ionized calcium greater than 5.6 mg/dL (1.40 mmol/L).</p>
        <p>The reference range for intact PTH varies by assay but is generally 10 to 65 pg/mL (1.1&#x02013;6.9 pmol/L). As approximately half of serum calcium is protein-bound, total calcium should be corrected for albumin (corrected calcium = measured calcium + 0.8 &#x000d7; [4 &#x02013; albumin]). Direct measurement of ionized calcium is preferred when albumin levels are abnormal or total calcium is borderline. In PHPT, serum phosphorus is usually low or low-normal due to PTH's phosphaturic action.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;After biochemical confirmation, further evaluation should assess end-organ involvement and exclude secondary causes (see <bold>Table 2</bold>). The initial laboratory assessment should include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Intact PTH&#x000a0;(by immunochemiluminometric or immunoradiometric assay)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Total and ionized calcium</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Serum phosphorus, albumin, alkaline phosphatase, and creatinine (to calculate eGFR or creatinine clearance)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D (levels should be repleted to greater than 30 ng/mL to exclude vitamin D deficiency as a secondary contributor to elevated PTH)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Urinary 24-hour calcium-to-creatinine ratio (assesses calcium excretion and differentiates PHPT from familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia [FHH], a calcium-to-creatinine clearance ratio &#x0003c;0.01 favors FHH, while a ratio &#x0003e;0.02 supports PHPT) Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/21613">Familial Hypocalciuric Hypercalcemia</ext-link>," for further information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r26">[26]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>If patients are taking biotin, lithium, or thiazide diuretics, these medications should be discontinued for several weeks before retesting to prevent laboratory interference or drug-induced alterations in calcium metabolism.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Skeletal and Renal Imaging</bold>
</p>
        <p>Skeletal and renal assessments are not required for diagnosis but may provide valuable adjunctive information. Dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry (DXA) should be performed at the lumbar spine, hip, and distal one-third radius to evaluate bone mineral density (BMD). Imaging for vertebral fractures,&#x000a0;eg, vertebral fracture assessment or plain x-rays, may also be indicated. Renal evaluation includes 24-hour urinary calcium measurement and renal imaging, eg, ultrasound, noncontrast computed tomography (CT), or KUB x-ray, to screen for nephrolithiasis or nephrocalcinosis, which may be clinically silent in up to 20% of patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Localizing Imaging</bold>
</p>
        <p>Localization imaging is not necessary for diagnosis, but is essential for preoperative planning and should be ordered by the operating surgeon. High-resolution neck ultrasonography and technetium-99m sestamibi scans, often combined with single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT), are standard first-line modalities. If these studies are inconclusive, 4-dimensional CT or F-18 fluorocholine positron emission tomography (PET)/CT may be utilized for greater sensitivity, particularly in cases of ectopic or multigland disease. Selective venous sampling is reserved for complex or reoperative cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;Additional information on localizing imaging techniques is provided below under surgical management.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Preoperative and Perioperative Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Laryngoscopy is recommended both before and after surgery to evaluate the function of the recurrent laryngeal nerve, particularly in reoperative cases or when voice changes are present. Baseline serum calcium and PTH levels should be measured on the day of surgery for comparison with intraoperative values. Vitamin D deficiency should be corrected preoperatively to reduce the risk of postoperative hypocalcemia or hungry bone syndrome.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Normocalcemic Primary Hyperparathyroidism</bold>
</p>
        <p>NPHPT is characterized by persistently elevated PTH levels with normal calcium concentrations. Diagnosis requires exclusion of all secondary causes of hyperparathyroidism, such as vitamin D deficiency, renal insufficiency (eGFR &#x0003c;60 mL/min/1.73 m&#x000b2;), calcium or magnesium deficiency, malabsorption, hypercalciuria, and specific medications (bisphosphonates, denosumab, lithium, proton pump inhibitors, SGLT2 inhibitors).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Normohormonal Primary Hyperparathyroidism</bold>
</p>
        <p>In NH-PHPT,&#x000a0;patients have hypercalcemia with PTH values within the laboratory reference range. In this presentation, PTH&#x000a0;is inappropriately normal given the degree of hypercalcemia. Evaluation follows the same principles as classic PHPT: confirm persistent hypercalcemia, rule out secondary causes (eg, medications, malignancy, vitamin D intoxication, or granulomatous disease), and assess renal and skeletal involvement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Parathyroid Crisis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Bone turnover markers and neurocognitive testing are not routinely recommended. However, when PHPT presents with severe or symptomatic hypercalcemia (&#x0003e;14 mg/dL), urgent evaluation for parathyroid crisis is necessary. Such cases often warrant inpatient management, aggressive medical therapy, and early surgical consultation. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/131067">Resistant Hypercalcemia</ext-link>," for further information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-23201.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 1. Summary of Evaluation for Primary Hyperparathyroidism</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 690px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;<bold>System Evaluation</bold></td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Recommended&#x000a0;Diagnostic Studies</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Reasoning</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Biochemical Evaluation</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Total and ionized serum calcium</p></list-item><list-item><p>Albumin (to calculate corrected calcium)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Intact PTH</p></list-item><list-item><p>Serum phosphorus</p></list-item><list-item><p>25-hydroxyvitamin D</p></list-item><list-item><p>Creatinine and eGFR</p></list-item><list-item><p>Alkaline phosphatase</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Confirms diagnosis and assesses metabolic profile</p></list-item><list-item><p>Elevated or inappropriately normal PTH with hypercalcemia confirms PHPT</p></list-item><list-item><p>Vitamin D should be &#x0003e;30 ng/mL to exclude secondary causes</p></list-item><list-item><p>Low or low-normal phosphorus supports PTH excess</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Urinary Evaluation</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>24-hour urinary calcium and creatinine</p></list-item><list-item><p>Calculate the calcium-to-creatinine clearance ratio</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Differentiates PHPT from familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH)</p></list-item><list-item><p>A ratio &#x0003c;0.01 favors FHH, while &#x0003e;0.02 supports PHPT</p></list-item><list-item><p>Also evaluates for hypercalciuria (&#x0003e;250 mg/day in women, &#x0003e;300 mg/day in men) and renal stone risk</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Skeletal Evaluation</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry (DXA) of lumbar spine, hip, and distal one-third radius</p></list-item><list-item><p>Vertebral imaging (x-ray or vertebral fracture assessment)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Evaluates bone mineral density and skeletal involvement.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cortical bone loss (distal radius) is common; vertebral imaging detects asymptomatic fractures.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Renal Evaluation</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>24-hour urinary calcium excretion</p></list-item><list-item><p>Renal imaging (ultrasound, noncontrast CT, or KUB x-ray)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Screens for nephrolithiasis or nephrocalcinosis, which may be subclinical in up to 20% of patients.</p></list-item><list-item><p>May also monitor renal function and risk of chronic kidney disease.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Localization (Preoperative Planning)</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Neck ultrasonography</p></list-item><list-item><p>Technetium-99m sestamibi scintigraphy &#x000b1; SPECT</p></list-item><list-item><p>4D CT or F-18 fluorocholine PET/CT (if prior imaging is inconclusive)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Used only for surgical planning, not diagnosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Identifies hyperfunctioning or ectopic parathyroid glands to guide minimally invasive parathyroidectomy</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Additional/Selective Tests</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>PTH-related peptide (PTHrP)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Genetic testing (if MEN syndromes suspected or FHH)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Used to rule out PTH-independent hypercalcemia or syndromic causes (eg, MEN1, MEN2A, <italic toggle="yes">CDC73</italic>-related disorders, FHH)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <table-wrap id="article-23201.table1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 2. Comparison of Classic, Normocalcemic, and Normohormonal Primary Hyperparathyroidism Features</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 690px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 119.359px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Diagnostic&#x000a0;Feature</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.297px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Classic</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 186.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Normocalcemic</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 185.562px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Normohormonal</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 119.359px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Serum Calcium</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.297px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Elevated (&#x0003e;10.2 mg/dL or &#x0003e;2.55 mmol/L)</td>
                <td style="width: 186.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Normal (confirmed total and ionized calcium)</td>
                <td style="width: 185.562px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Elevated (&#x0003e;10.2 mg/dL or &#x0003e;2.55 mmol/L)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 119.359px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>PTH</bold>
</td>
