                <td style="width: 186.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Persistently elevated on &#x02265;2 measurements (&#x02265;3&#x02013;6 months apart)</td>
                <td style="width: 185.562px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within normal range but inappropriately unsuppressed given hypercalcemia</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 119.359px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Phosphorus</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.297px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Low or low-normal</td>
                <td style="width: 186.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Low or low-normal</td>
                <td style="width: 185.562px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Low or low-normal</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 119.359px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Vitamin D (25-OH)</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.297px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Replete to &#x02265;30 ng/mL (75 nmol/L) before confirming diagnosis.</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 186.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Replete to &#x02265;30 ng/mL (75 nmol/L) before confirming diagnosis</td>
                <td style="width: 185.562px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Replete to &#x02265;30 ng/mL (75 nmol/L) before confirming diagnosis</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 119.359px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Renal Function (eGFR)</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.297px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Renal function usually normal; may decline with chronic disease</p></list-item><list-item><p>GFR should&#x000a0;be &#x02265;60 mL/min/1.73 m&#x000b2; to exclude secondary hyperparathyroidism</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 186.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Usually normal, may decline with chronic disease</p></list-item><list-item><p>Should&#x000a0;be &#x02265;60 mL/min/1.73 m&#x000b2; to exclude secondary hyperparathyroidism</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 185.562px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Usually normal, may decline with chronic disease</p></list-item><list-item><p>Should&#x000a0;be &#x02265;60 mL/min/1.73 m&#x000b2; to exclude secondary hyperparathyroidism</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 119.359px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Common Secondary Causes to Exclude</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.297px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Malignancy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Thiazides</p></list-item><list-item><p>Lithium</p></list-item><list-item><p>Vitamin D intoxication</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 186.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Vitamin D deficiency</p></list-item><list-item><p>Renal insufficiency</p></list-item><list-item><p>Malabsorption</p></list-item><list-item><p>SGLT2i</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bisphosphonates</p></list-item><list-item><p>Denosumab</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 185.562px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Malignancy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Medications</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hyperthyroidism</p></list-item><list-item><p>Adrenal insufficiency</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 119.359px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Typical Clinical Presentation</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.297px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Asymptomatic hypercalcemia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Symptomatic nephrolithiasis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Osteoporosis</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 186.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Often identified during bone or renal evaluation with normal calcium.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 185.562px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Incidental hypercalcemia with normal PTH</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 119.359px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Imaging Findings</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.297px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Single adenoma (approximately 80%)</td>
                <td style="width: 186.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Often a single adenoma</p></list-item><list-item><p>Occasionally multigland hyperplasia</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 185.562px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Higher rate of multigland disease</p></list-item><list-item><p>May require bilateral neck exploration</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Surgical intervention remains the sole definitive treatment for PHPT and is recommended for all eligible patients, irrespective of age or symptomatology, once diagnostic criteria are fulfilled. Parathyroidectomy achieves cure rates above 95% in experienced centers and improves bone mineral density, renal function, and overall quality of life. Observation and medical management are reserved for asymptomatic individuals, those with mild biochemical abnormalities, or patients deemed unsuitable for surgery due to comorbidities or elevated anesthesia risk (see <bold>Table 3</bold>).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r35">[35]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical Management&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Indications for surgery</bold>
</p>
        <p>Surgical intervention is indicated for all symptomatic patients, including those presenting with nephrolithiasis, nephrocalcinosis, fragility fractures, or osteoporosis. Additionally, surgery may be considered for individuals exhibiting neurocognitive or neuropsychiatric symptoms attributable to PHPT, as well as for those who prefer definitive management.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Asymptomatic patients&#x000a0;who meet any of the following criteria are also eligible for parathyroidectomy:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Age &#x0003c;50 years</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Serum calcium &#x0003e;1.0 mg/dL (0.25 mmol/L) above the upper limit of normal</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Creatinine clearance &#x0003c;60 mL/min or eGFR &#x0003c;60 mL/min/1.73 m&#x000b2;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Bone mineral density T-score &#x02264;&#x02013;2.5 at any site (eg, lumbar spine, hip, or distal radius)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Presence of vertebral fracture on imaging</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Renal stones or calcifications on ultrasound or CT</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypercalciuria (&#x0003e;250 mg/24 hours in women and &#x0003e;300 mg/24 hours in men)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Preoperative localization</bold>
</p>
        <p>Preoperative imaging is performed only for surgical planning, not for diagnosis, as biochemical testing confirms the disease. The following localization studies help guide the choice between focused or bilateral exploration and minimize operative time:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neck ultrasonography:&#x000a0;</bold>High-resolution neck ultrasound provides anatomical visualization of the parathyroid glands and is widely available. The sensitivity of ultrasound for identifying abnormal parathyroid glands typically ranges from 70% to 81%, with specificity between 90% and 95%. Sensitivity is reduced in cases of multiglandular disease, small adenomas, posteriorly located glands, or ectopic mediastinal lesions. Ultrasound is also operator-dependent, yields no functional information, and is less reliable for ectopic, mediastinal, or posteriorly situated glands.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r3">[3]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Technetium-99m sestamibi (MIBI) scintigraphy:&#x000a0;</bold>MIBI scans (planar scintigraphy or SPECT/CT) assess enriched mitochondrial activity in hyperfunctioning parathyroid tissue, giving functional information.&#x000a0;Reported sensitivity ranges from 64% to 91%, depending on the imaging protocol, gland size, and presence of multigland disease, while specificity ranges from 83% to 96%.&#x000a0;Planar MIBI imaging alone has lower sensitivity (approximately 42%&#x02013;79%).&#x000a0;SPECT/CT, which combines functional and anatomic imaging, significantly improves localization, achieving sensitivities up to 97% and high specificity. Sensitivity is reduced in multiglandular hyperplasia, small (&#x0003c;500 mg) adenomas, and cystic or normohormonal disease variants.&#x000a0;When&#x000a0;combined with neck ultrasound, the sensitivity for localization exceeds 90%, and in high-volume centers can reach as high as 96%, with specificity between 83% and 94%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r3">[3]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Four-dimensional (4D) CT</bold>: This modality provides detailed anatomic and perfusion data, especially as a second-line modality after inconclusive or discordant ultrasound or sestamibi scans. It has an average sensitivity of 80% and a specificity of 84%.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>F-18 fluorocholine positron emission tomography (PET/CT)</bold>:&#x000a0;PET/CT offers high sensitivity for small or ectopic adenomas and may be used in reoperative or complex cases.&#x000a0;Large prospective and retrospective studies report sensitivity values of 93% to 99% and specificity of 78% to 91%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r38">[38]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Intraoperative PTH monitoring&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Intraoperative PTH monitoring provides biochemical confirmation of surgical success and reduces the risk of persistent or recurrent disease. PTH has a half-life of 3 to 5 minutes. Therefore, a 50% or greater reduction from baseline within 10 to 20 minutes after gland excision indicates successful removal of all hypersecreting tissue. A final PTH value falling into the normal range further supports biochemical cure. If the expected drop does not occur, exploration continues to identify additional hyperfunctioning glands. Routine intraoperative PTH monitoring is particularly useful in minimally invasive and reoperative surgeries, as it allows real-time decision-making and minimizes unnecessary dissection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r35">[35]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Minimally invasive parathyroidectomy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Minimally invasive parathyroidectomy (MIP) is the preferred surgical approach when preoperative imaging localizes a single adenoma. The technique uses a small incision (2&#x02013;3 cm) and is explicitly directed at the abnormal gland, allowing less tissue dissection, reduced operative time, a shorter hospital stay, and a faster recovery. MIP may be performed under local, regional, or general anesthesia, depending on the patient's and surgeon's preference. Intraoperative PTH monitoring (see below) enhances success rates and confirms cure in real time. Conversion to bilateral exploration may be required if intraoperative findings suggest multiglandular disease or if intraoperative PTH levels fail to fall appropriately.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Bilateral neck exploration&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Bilateral neck exploration&#x000a0;remains the gold standard for cases where localization studies are negative, inconclusive, or suggest multiglandular hyperplasia. Bilateral neck exploration is also indicated in patients with familial syndromes (eg, MEN1, MEN2A) or lithium-associated PHPT, which are associated with multigland disease. During bilateral neck exploration, all 4 parathyroid glands are identified and evaluated for enlargement or nodularity with the goal of excising all hyperfunctioning tissue while preserving at least 1 viable normal gland to prevent permanent hypoparathyroidism.&#x000a0;Although&#x000a0;bilateral neck exploration is more extensive than MIP, it remains a safe and curative option in experienced hands.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r35">[35]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Special surgical considerations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Special consideration is required in the following clinical settings:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Pregnancy</bold>: Parathyroidectomy can be performed safely in the second trimester when medically indicated.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Reoperative surgery</bold>: Should be performed only by experienced endocrine surgeons and requires positive preoperative localization.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Parathyroid carcinoma</bold>: En bloc resection with ipsilateral thyroid lobectomy and surrounding soft tissue is required to achieve negative margins.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/26659">Parathyroid Cancer</ext-link>," for further information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r20">[20]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Postoperative Considerations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Postoperative care includes monitoring for hypocalcemia and hungry bone syndrome. Hungry bone syndrome occurs in up to 10% of cases, particularly after long-standing disease or severe hypercalcemia. Management includes high-dose oral or IV calcium and active vitamin D (calcitriol) replacement. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/79790">Hungry Bone Syndrome</ext-link>," for further information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r39">[39]</xref> Transient hypocalcemia occurs in approximately 50% of patients and should be managed with supplementation as needed.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Medical Management and Surveillance</bold>
</p>
        <p>For patients with PHPT&#x000a0;who are not surgical candidates or who elect nonoperative management, the primary goals are to control hypercalcemia, preserve bone density, and monitor for renal complications. Calcium intake should be maintained within the normal dietary range (1,000&#x02013;1,200 mg/day) to prevent secondary PTH stimulation. Vitamin D deficiency should be corrected to achieve a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level &#x0003e;30 ng/mL (75 nmol/L), which can reduce PTH levels and mitigate bone loss. Cinacalcet, a calcimimetic that activates the calcium-sensing receptor, effectively lowers serum calcium in over 70% of patients but has minimal effect on BMD. Bisphosphonates (eg, alendronate) and denosumab can increase BMD, particularly at cortical sites, but do not normalize calcium. When both hypercalcemia and osteoporosis coexist, combination therapy with cinacalcet and an antiresorptive agent is appropriate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients managed medically require regular surveillance, including annual serum calcium, creatinine, or eGFR, and vitamin D testing. PTH should be repeated as clinically indicated. DXA scans of the lumbar spine, hip, and distal radius every 1 to 2 years should be used to monitor osteoporosis. Renal imaging or 24-hour urinary calcium&#x000a0;should be obtained if nephrolithiasis is suspected. Surgery should be reconsidered if the patient develops symptoms, worsening hypercalcemia (&#x0003e;1 mg/dL above normal), nephrolithiasis, significant bone loss (T-score &#x02264; &#x02013;2.5), or declining renal function.&#x000a0;</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-23201.table2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 3. Overview of Management Pathway</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 690px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Patient Category</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Recommended Management</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Goal</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Symptomatic PHPT</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Parathyroidectomy</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Definitive cure; &#x0003e;95% success</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Asymptomatic PHPT&#x000a0;</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Parathyroidectomy</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Prevent bone and renal complications</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mild disease, surgical contraindication</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Observation plus annual monitoring</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Track calcium, BMD, renal status</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Hypercalcemia (surgical contraindication)</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cinacalcet</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reduce serum calcium</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Osteoporosis (surgical contraindication)</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Bisphosphonates/Denosumab</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Improve BMD</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Severe hypercalcemic crisis</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">IV bisphosphonate, denosumab, hydration</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Short-term control</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of PHPT includes numerous disorders characterized by hypercalcemia or elevated PTH levels. Accurate diagnosis relies on a comprehensive biochemical evaluation to differentiate PTH-dependent from PTH-independent causes of hypercalcemia.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Secondary and Tertiary Hyperparathyroidism</bold>
</p>
        <p>Secondary hyperparathyroidism arises as a compensatory mechanism in response to chronic hypocalcemia or vitamin D deficiency. Common etiologies include chronic kidney disease (CKD), vitamin D deficiency, and malabsorption syndromes. In secondary hyperparathyroidism, calcium levels are low or normal, PTH is elevated, and phosphate is elevated in CKD or decreased in vitamin D deficiency. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23202">Secondary Hyperparathyroidism</ext-link>," for further information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r42">[42]</xref></p>
        <p>Tertiary hyperparathyroidism develops following extended secondary hyperparathyroidism, particularly in patients undergoing dialysis, when parathyroid glands begin to secrete PTH autonomously. This condition leads to hypercalcemia and multiglandular hyperplasia. The simultaneous presence of elevated PTH and hypercalcemia distinguishes tertiary hyperparathyroidism from secondary hyperparathyroidism (elevated PTH, low or normal calcium) and from primary hyperparathyroidism (elevated PTH, hypercalcemia, but without preceding renal disease).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Normocalcemic Primary Hyperparathyroidism</bold>
</p>
        <p>NPHPT is defined by persistently elevated PTH with normal total and ionized calcium levels, confirmed on repeated measurements. Diagnosis requires exclusion of all secondary causes, eg, vitamin D deficiency, renal insufficiency, inadequate calcium intake, hypercalciuria, magnesium deficiency, or medication effects. Patients may present with subtle skeletal or renal manifestations. Longitudinal studies demonstrate that up to 20% of cases progress to classical hypercalcemic PHPT. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23200">Normocalcemic Hyperparathyroidism</ext-link>," for further information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Normohormonal Primary Hyperparathyroidism&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>NH-PHPT is characterized by biochemical hypercalcemia with PTH levels that remain within the normal range but are inappropriately unsuppressed in the context of elevated calcium. In nonparathyroid causes of hypercalcemia, PTH is typically suppressed. Patients often present with single-gland disease, although the incidence of multiglandular involvement is higher than in classic PHPT. Intraoperative assessment frequently requires bilateral neck exploration, and preoperative localization is less sensitive. Despite these challenges, surgical cure rates exceed 90%. Diagnosis relies on recognizing that normal PTH levels in the setting of hypercalcemia are physiologically inappropriate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Familial Hypocalciuric Hypercalcemia</bold>
</p>
        <p>FHH is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by inactivating mutations in the <italic toggle="yes">CaSR</italic> gene. These mutations result in an increased calcium set point and reduced urinary calcium excretion. Clinical features include lifelong mild hypercalcemia, low urinary calcium excretion (&#x0003c;100 mg/24 hours), a calcium&#x02013;creatinine clearance ratio below 0.01, and normal or mildly elevated PTH levels. Parathyroidectomy is ineffective and therefore not recommended. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/21613">Familial Hypocalciuric Hypercalcemia</ext-link>," for further information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Malignancy-Related Hypercalcemia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Malignancy is a major PTH-independent cause of hypercalcemia, affecting 20% to 30% of cancer patients. Most cases are due to the secretion of parathyroid hormone-related protein (PTHrP) by squamous cell, renal, breast, or bladder carcinomas. Other mechanisms include osteolytic metastases and cytokine-mediated bone resorption, as seen in multiple myeloma. Laboratory findings typically show elevated calcium, suppressed PTH, increased PTHrP, and normal or elevated phosphate. Diagnosis is confirmed by identifying low intact PTH and high PTHrP levels.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/41122">Malignancy-Related Hypercalcemia</ext-link>," for further information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r43">[43]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Parathyroid Carcinoma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Parathyroid carcinoma is a rare malignancy, representing less than 1% of primary hyperparathyroidism cases. Patients typically present with markedly elevated calcium levels (&#x0003e;14 mg/dL) and PTH concentrations&#x000a0;5 to 10 times the upper normal limit, often with neck masses, hoarseness, or recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement. Clinical severity is primarily due to severe, refractory hypercalcemia rather than local tumor invasion. Histological confirmation requires evidence of capsular or vascular invasion. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/26659">Parathyroid Cancer</ext-link>," for further information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Parathyromatosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Parathyromatosis is an uncommon cause of persistent or recurrent hyperparathyroidism, most frequently resulting from seeding of parathyroid tissue during surgical procedures or ablation, although it may rarely be developmental. The condition is characterized by multiple parathyroid tissue nodules embedded within fibrotic tissue, which can mimic carcinoma but lack true malignant invasion. Management is challenging; complete excision is potentially curative but technically demanding. Calcimimetics and denosumab may be used to control symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r44">[44]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Genetic Syndromes Associated with Primary Hyperparathyroidism</bold>
</p>
        <p>Approximately 10% of primary hyperparathyroidism cases are genetic in origin and often involve multiple glands. Genetic testing is advised for patients younger than 30 years, those with a family history, or individuals with recurrent or multiglandular disease, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>MEN1 (<italic toggle="yes">MEN1</italic> mutation): Pituitary and pancreatic tumors; &#x0003e;90% develop PHPT&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r45">[45]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>MEN2A (<italic toggle="yes">RET</italic> mutation): Medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, and parathyroid hyperplasia&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r46">[46]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism: Homozygous calcium-sensing receptor (<italic toggle="yes">CaSR</italic>) mutations causing life-threatening hypercalcemia in infancy <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r47">[47]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Iatrogenic and Endocrine Causes</bold>
</p>
        <p>Precise differentiation from true PHPT is essential to avoid unnecessary surgical intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Several medications and metabolic disorders can mimic or exacerbate PHPT by inducing hypercalcemia with elevated or inappropriately normal PTH levels, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Lithium therapy</bold>: Lithium reduces CaSR sensitivity, increasing the calcium threshold required for PTH suppression. This may result in parathyroid hyperplasia and, over time, autonomous PTH secretion that biochemically resembles PHPT. In most cases, hypercalcemia resolves after discontinuation of lithium; however, persistent cases may necessitate surgical intervention.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Thiazide diuretics</bold>: Thiazides enhance distal tubular calcium reabsorption, which can result in mild hypercalcemia. This effect may reveal previously unrecognized or subclinical PHPT rather than directly causing the condition. In most cases, PTH levels are normal or only slightly elevated.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Biotin interference</bold>: High-dose biotin supplementation (greater than 5 mg per day) may result in falsely low PTH measurements, potentially leading to misdiagnosis or missed diagnosis of primary hyperparathyroidism. Biotin should be discontinued at least 48 hours before testing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r48">[48]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Vitamin toxicity</bold>: Excessive vitamin D raises both calcium and phosphate levels due to increased intestinal absorption, with suppressed PTH. Vitamin A toxicity increases bone resorption, leading to hypercalcemia and low PTH. Both are PTH-independent causes that can be distinguished by elevated 25-hydroxyvitamin D or retinol levels.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Milk-alkali syndrome</bold>: Excessive intake of calcium carbonate or calcium supplements can cause milk-alkali syndrome, characterized by hypercalcemia, metabolic alkalosis, and renal insufficiency.&#x000a0;PTH is typically suppressed, distinguishing it from PHPT unless an underlying parathyroid disorder coexists.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Granulomatous diseases</bold> (eg, sarcoidosis, tuberculosis): Extrarenal 1&#x003b1;-hydroxylase activity&#x000a0;converts 25-hydroxyvitamin D to active calcitriol, stimulating calcium absorption. This leads to elevated calcium and calcitriol, suppressed PTH, and normal or elevated phosphate levels.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Endocrine mimickers</bold>
<bold>(hyperthyroidism, adrenal insufficiency, and rebound hypercalcemia)</bold>:&#x000a0;Hyperthyroidism accelerates bone turnover and calcium release, causing mild hypercalcemia with low PTH.&#x000a0;Adrenal insufficiency may cause transient hypercalcemia due to volume depletion.&#x000a0;Rebound hypercalcemia after denosumab withdrawal, or use of PTH analogs (teriparatide, abaloparatide), can also raise calcium but are PTH-independent and typically transient.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r4">[4]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis for PHPT is generally favorable, especially among patients with mild or asymptomatic disease. The severity of hypercalcemia influences long-term outcomes, the presence of complications, and the chosen treatment strategy. Most asymptomatic individuals remain stable for extended periods and have a normal life expectancy. In contrast, patients with moderate to severe hypercalcemia (serum calcium &#x02265;10.8 mg/dL) or complications, eg, nephrolithiasis, osteoporosis, or fragility fractures, face a higher risk of morbidity and mortality. In these cases, surgical intervention substantially improves survival rates.</p>
        <p>Parathyroidectomy achieves a cure in more than 95% of cases when performed by experienced endocrine surgeons. This procedure results in normalization of calcium and PTH levels, enhanced BMD, and decreased incidence of kidney stones and fractures. These skeletal benefits are sustained for over a decade after successful surgery. Among patients managed conservatively, most maintain stable calcium levels and BMD; however, approximately 25% to 40% experience disease progression or develop new indications for surgery within 10 to 15 years, particularly younger patients or those with higher initial calcium concentrations.&#x000a0;In general, the long-term prognosis is excellent for patients who receive timely diagnosis, appropriate surgical intervention when indicated, and consistent monitoring of calcium levels, renal function, and bone health.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>
<bold>Complications of Untreated Primary Hyperparathyroidism</bold>
</p>
        <p>Complications of PHPT result from chronic hypercalcemia and excessive PTH activity, impacting the skeletal, renal, neurocognitive, cardiovascular, and gastrointestinal systems. Skeletal complications are particularly significant and include progressive loss of BMD, especially at cortical sites. This loss leads to osteopenia, osteoporosis, fractures, and osteitis fibrosa cystica, which manifests as bone pain and &#x0201c;brown tumors.&#x0201d; Parathyroidectomy can reverse these skeletal effects, with BMD increasing by up to 10% within the first year and continuing to improve for more than a decade following surgery.</p>
        <p>Renal complications arise from PTH-mediated calcium mobilization and hypercalciuria, which increase the risk of nephrolithiasis, nephrocalcinosis, and chronic kidney disease. Up to 50% of symptomatic patients develop kidney stones, and 20% may progress to nephrocalcinosis without treatment. Surgical intervention significantly reduces the recurrence of kidney stones and helps stabilize renal function.</p>
        <p>Neuropsychiatric manifestations, including fatigue, depression, anxiety, cognitive slowing, and muscle weakness, are common and may improve following parathyroidectomy, although evidence for this improvement remains mixed. Chronic hypercalcemia can also cause gastrointestinal symptoms, eg, constipation, nausea, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, pancreatitis, or peptic ulcer disease, particularly in patients with MEN1-associated gastrinoma.</p>
        <p>Cardiovascular complications are more common in moderate to severe disease and may include hypertension, vascular calcification, left ventricular hypertrophy, and increased arterial stiffness, although the causal relationship is not well established. Surgical correction reduces calcium and PTH levels, but evidence regarding long-term reductions in cardiovascular risk remains inconsistent. Chronic hypercalcemia can also cause band keratopathy and soft-tissue calcifications.</p>
        <p>In the absence of surgical intervention, approximately one-quarter to one-third of patients experience disease progression over 10 to 15 years, developing worsening hypercalcemia, bone loss, or renal complications. In rare instances, hypercalcemic crisis may occur, characterized by severe hypercalcemia (&#x0003e;14 mg/dL), dehydration, altered mental status, and arrhythmias, necessitating urgent treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Complications Related to Surgery</bold>
</p>
        <p>Although parathyroidectomy is curative in more than 95% of patients, it carries inherent surgical risks. Common postoperative complications include transient hypocalcemia, which may result from hungry bone syndrome. This syndrome is characterized by profound hypocalcemia with hypophosphatemia and elevated alkaline phosphatase following rapid skeletal remineralization. Additional complications include permanent hypoparathyroidism (1%&#x02013;3%), recurrent laryngeal nerve injury (1%&#x02013;2%), and, rarely, neck hematoma or persistent hyperparathyroidism due to missed multigland disease. Despite these risks, the benefits of surgery in appropriate candidates,&#x000a0;eg, normalized calcium, improved bone and renal outcomes, and reduced long-term morbidity, outweigh the potential complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23201.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Deterrence and patient education play a central role in reducing complications and disease progression associated with PHPT. Patients benefit from a clear explanation of parathyroid function and the effects of excess parathyroid hormone on calcium homeostasis, bone health, and kidney function. Education should emphasize that PHPT often causes few or no symptoms, which makes routine laboratory testing and follow-up essential even when patients feel well. Counseling on maintaining adequate hydration, avoiding excessive calcium supplementation unless prescribed, and adhering to the recommended vitamin D repletion supports stable calcium metabolism and reduces the risk of kidney stones. Patients should also understand the importance of reporting symptoms, eg, fatigue, muscle weakness, bone pain, or renal colic, promptly.</p>
        <p>Patient education should reinforce that parathyroidectomy offers a definitive cure for most individuals and prevents long-term skeletal and renal complications. For patients managed with observation, clinicians should stress the need for regular monitoring of serum calcium, kidney function, and bone density to detect progression early. Clear communication regarding the excellent prognosis, low surgical risk in experienced centers, and benefits of timely intervention improves shared decision-making, adherence to follow-up, and long-term outcomes, allowing most patients with PHPT to maintain healthy, normal lives.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Successful management of PHPT depends on recognizing mild or masked cases, accurately distinguishing primary from secondary causes, and selecting appropriate surgical candidates. Parathyroidectomy remains the only curative therapy.</p>
        <p>Key factors to bear in mind when managing&#x000a0;PHPT include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Misclassification:</bold> Not correcting calcium for albumin or failing to measure ionized calcium can obscure the diagnosis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Vitamin D deficiency:</bold> Failure to replete can falsely elevate PTH and mimic PHPT.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Missed cortical bone loss:</bold> Omitting distal radius DEXA can underestimate bone risk.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Biotin interference:</bold> High-dose supplements can yield falsely low PTH values. Stop biotin 48 hours or more before testing.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Incomplete family history:</bold> Overlooking familial syndromes (MEN, FHH) may lead to unnecessary surgery.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>PHPT is a common endocrine disorder caused by autonomous overproduction of parathyroid hormone, leading to hypercalcemia and disruption of calcium homeostasis. Most cases result from a single benign parathyroid adenoma, while multigland disease and carcinoma occur less frequently. Increased use of routine biochemical screening has shifted presentation toward asymptomatic hypercalcemia, including normocalcemic and normohormonal variants. Diagnosis relies on biochemical testing that demonstrates elevated or inappropriately normal parathyroid hormone levels in the presence of hypercalcemia after secondary causes have been excluded. Parathyroidectomy provides definitive cure in most patients, while selected individuals may be managed with observation or medical therapy and structured surveillance.</p>
        <p>Effective management of PHPT depends on coordinated interprofessional collaboration. Physicians and advanced practitioners oversee diagnosis and treatment by interpreting calcium and PTH levels, differentiating PHPT from secondary causes, and assessing surgical eligibility. Endocrine surgeons perform parathyroidectomy, while anesthesiologists, nurses, and perioperative staff maintain patient safety and monitor for complications such as postoperative hypocalcemia. Radiologists and nuclear medicine specialists localize affected glands. Pharmacists review medications that influence calcium balance, guide supplementation, and prevent drug interactions. Dietitians provide nutritional counseling to support appropriate calcium and vitamin D intake, and laboratory staff ensure accurate testing.</p>
        <p>Clear interprofessional communication and care coordination are essential to optimize outcomes, minimize complications, and ensure continuity from diagnosis through follow-up. Collaborative, patient-centered decision-making, especially regarding surgery versus surveillance, should respect patient preferences. Ongoing education, shared documentation, and multidisciplinary discussions enhance safety, prevent errors, and support a unified approach to long-term management, improving bone health, kidney function, and overall quality of life for patients with PHPT.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23201.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
